hi there Steve coffin here I'm back from  my travels back in Vancouver and happy  to be home today I'm gonna talk about  learning styles and in particular visual  learners before that I just want to  again relay to you just how satisfying  it is to be able to speak many different  languages when I was in London I don't  think there was a single sales clerk in  any store or a restaurant waiter who was  British so I was able to communicate  with people in you know Greek Romanian  even some Arabic even some Farsi but of  course and Spanish French Italian but  Ukrainian even I got an extra Easter in  this square because I spoke to the  fellow who was shucking the oysters in  Ukrainian  but of course my Greek and my Romanian  are lacking and not a problem because I  have studied them I have the resources  the many stories that I listen to and  read so many many times they're in my  brain somewhere they're also in link I  can go review them and they'll come back  to me and I'm confident that every bit  of effort I put into those languages is  gonna come back but it sort of relates  again to to the theme and that is that  language learning is is really to my  mind as I've said many times it's  primarily about input if you with enough  input and enough comprehension and the  sufficient vocabulary you will  eventually be able to speak you don't  need to worry about what you can speak  now day one week one week ten or  whatever but that this process of  acquiring a language is one of  associating meaning with the sounds of  words and the written word to me that is  the sort of connections that have to be  made and the sort of visual strategy  which I had for example starting into  Arabic they'd have a picture of a camel  or a picture of a chicken or a picture  of an apple and relate that to a word  written in Arabic I find that that  doesn't help very much the visual  representation of what we're learning of  words is very limited for a variety of  reasons first  because there's only so much that you  can represent visually you know feelings  are much more difficult to represent  that way and second of all because  ultimately the connection the visual  connection is with a written word that's  the most useful thing because you have  to eventually be able to read and if  you're looking at a word and all you can  think of is you know dog then you're not  really reading the letters that are  there and I know I struggle with my  Arabic and not my Farsi I struggle with  getting used to the writing system it's  not a matter of theoretically knowing  what the letters represent it's actually  being able to comfortably read and I am  getting I'm slowly getting better at it  and I find that as a consequence I hear  the words more clearly when I listen so  there's a real connection between what  you hear and what you see written and in  fact there is I should point out by the  way that there is lots of research over  the last 10 years that points out that  learning styles essentially don't exist  you just have to google debunking  learning styles and you'll find ample  articles on that subject  also I'm gonna leave a link here in the  description box to an article that  explains that the part of the brain that  is used when we read is the same part of  the brain it's the same function as when  we listen to words so it's the same  you're reinforcing that same sort of  process the ability to make sense to  make derive meaning out of the written  word and the words that you hear and so  and so now I'm finding and of course  because Farsi is as a language more  similar to other European languages it's  easier than Arabic and also in Farsi the  writing system is a little more  consistent than it is in Arabic in other  words I am more likely to be able to  pronounce a Farsi word correctly than an  Arabic word because there are fewer you  know like son letters and moon letters  and other inconsistencies that we find  in Arabic don't exist in Farsi however  by doing the Farsi and reading in Farsi  it's all contributing to me being able  to read those letters better and better  as I read my Arabic I find that both of  them are sort of reinforcing the each  one is reinforcing the other but the  process is very much one of connecting  written words to spoken words that  that's the process and as I improve my  ability to do that as I hear the  language more and more clearly as I can  read more and more easily as I can more  easily derive meaning as I'm reading and  listening all of this is thus  snowballing improvement in acquiring the  language and I don't think there is  anyone who in the research suggests that  some people may prefer to watch videos  or may prefer to look at pictures but in  terms of how we learn there is no real  difference and we all have to learn to  read I shouldn't say we have to we can  learn without reading but reading is  such a powerful way to it to acquire  information in any language and a  powerful way to increase your vocabulary  and the short path to improve reading  skills is to focus on this connection  between the written word and the sound  of that word and even in languages like  I mean English is not as consistent as  Spanish so therefore it's more difficult  and Arabic is even more difficult  however if you do enough reading and  enough listening you start to associate  certain words with certain sounds it may  not always be possible in Arabic to  reproduce the sound if you haven't seen  that word before  but eventually as we read more and more  we have to get used to these words we've  seen them before and so I just wanted to  sort of make this point that that the  visual connection that I make  increasingly in a language is between  the written word and the sound of that  word and then eventually to the meaning  of that word and this goes beyond you  know red green car house door it also  goes as far  as the sort of grammatical structures  once we get used to the fact that  certain ideas are expressed a certain  way and if that expression of the idea  is grammatically correct that's then  what I cling to not some reference back  to a rule that tells me that this is  this case or this is some other rule so  just to say that when it comes to visual  learning some of you may think your  visual learners I challenge you I don't  think that's the case I think we all  have to follow more or less the same  strategies when we acquire a language so  and I you know I look forward to the  usual comments and criticisms bye for  now  [Music]
 

